WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.320
Industrial network telemetry describes communication among HMIs, PLCs, RTUs,

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:10.660
SCADA servers, historians, engineering workstations, gateways, and remote-access systems.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:15.410
It can show source and destination, protocol, direction, timing, volume, session behavior,

00:00:15.410 --> 00:00:18.380
and in some cases the industrial operation represented.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.125
Stable OT networks often produce recognizable patterns.

00:00:21.125 --> 00:00:24.040
An HMI polls a controller, a historian collects values,

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:27.971
and an engineering workstation communicates during approved maintenance.

00:00:27.971 --> 00:00:30.855
New peers, changed frequency, unexpected protocols,

00:00:30.855 --> 00:00:35.310
or traffic crossing an unintended boundary can become useful investigation signals.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.480
Passive collection is commonly used to reduce disruption risk.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.400
Network taps or switch mirror ports copy existing traffic to a sensor without asking

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.600
devices to respond.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.160
Placement matters: sensors near industrial DMZs, control-area boundaries, remote links,

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.480
and key supervisory paths see different parts of the system.

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.670
Collection design should account for redundancy, bandwidth, management access,

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:55.360
and failure.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.205
A monitoring sensor should not become part of the control path or create an uncontrolled

00:00:59.205 --> 00:00:59.770
bridge.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:04.240
Captured data also reveals sensitive architecture and should be protected accordingly.

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.690
Network telemetry has blind spots.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:10.150
It may miss serial segments, encrypted content, dormant devices, local actions,

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.880
traffic inside an unobserved switch, or devices hidden behind a gateway.

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:16.760
Metadata can still be valuable when content is unavailable,

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.190
but confidence must reflect the actual coverage.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:21.940
Interpret communication with asset and process context.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:24.300
A protocol write is not automatically malicious,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.990
and a missing connection may reflect a planned shutdown.

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:30.610
Network evidence becomes actionable when it is combined with maintenance records,

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:34.100
device roles, endpoint logs, process trends, and engineering knowledge.
